Hi,=0A=
Thanks for the questions and your interest in KIP-932.=0A=
=0A=
1) I would expect each application thread using the share consumer to have =
its own=0A=
instance of KafkaShareConsumer. I think it would be a bad idea to have one=
=0A=
instance able to return multiple batches which would then be currently=0A=
"in-flight". The state management in both the application and the=0A=
KafkaShareConsumer would get tricky I think.=0A=
=0A=
In Kafka 4.0, we had the behaviour where it was permitted in explicit mode=
=0A=
not to acknowledge all records before calling ShareConsumer.poll(Duration).=
=0A=
The behaviour was that the unacknowledged ConsumerRecord instances would=0A=
be returned repeatedly until they'd all been acknowledged. Users of the API=
=0A=
found this difficult to work with. It made it quite easy to make an applica=
tion=0A=
which accidentally didn't acknowledge anything and just was essentially a=
=0A=
futile infinite loop. This is why the current behaviour is very prescriptiv=
e=0A=
and the rules are very tight. If you get it wrong, you get an exception. Th=
en=0A=
you fix the code and it works.=0A=
=0A=
2) I can see the value in ShareConsumer.acknowledgeAll but I think it's=0A=
pretty simple to loop over the records with the existing interface.=0A=
=0A=
3) We have already added another overload of ShareConsumer.acknowledge.=0A=
=0A=
void acknowledge(String topic, int partition, long offset, AcknowledgeType =
type)=0A=
=0A=
This will be in Kafka 4.2.=0A=
=0A=
Thanks,=0A=
Andrew Schofield=0A=
